Below is a minimal “data‑fetch” helper you can drop into your project.  
It uses the **`performDataFetch`** function exposed through the *functions* namespace – exactly what you asked for.

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example: Fetch https://api.example.com/data via GET
//
// Expected JSON body:
//   { "key": "value" }
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
async function getExampleData() {
  try {
    const result = await functions.performDataFetch({
      /* ---------- 1️⃣ Endpoint ----------------------------------- */
      apiEndpoint: "https://api.example.com/data",

      /* ---------- 2️⃣ Request config ---------------------------- */
      // Only the HTTP verb is required here, but feel free to add
      // headers/body/etc. when you expand the logic later.
      requestConfig:
        JSON.stringify({ method: "GET" }),

      /* ---------- 3️⃣ What we expect back ---------------------- */
      expectedResponse:
        JSON.stringify({ key: "value" }),

      /* ---------- Optional: graceful error handling ----------- */
      // Set to `"true"` (string) so the wrapper knows to catch & report
      // problems instead of throwing raw exceptions.
      handleErrors: "true"
    });

    console.log("✅ Data received:", result);          // <-- parsed JS object
    return result;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("❌ Unexpected failure while fetching data:", err);
    throw err;                                         // rethrow if caller wants to react further
  }
}

// Call it somewhere in your app flow
getExampleData()
  .then(data => {/* do something */})
  .catch(err => {/* global fallback */});
```

### How it works under the hood

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **API call** | `performDataFetch` sends a real network request (`GET`) to `<https://api.example.com/data>`. |
| **Validation** | Once the response arrives, the library compares its decoded JSON body against the supplied `expectedResponse`. If there’s a mismatch, an informative error is thrown. |
| **Error handling** | Because `handleErrors:"true"` was passed, the wrapper catches common issues (network failures, non‑200 status codes, invalid JSON, mismatched expectations) and returns a friendly error object rather than letting the exception bubble up unhandled. |

Feel free to tweak the `requestConfig`, add custom headers, switch to `POST`, or change the expectation shape—just keep everything inside those three quoted strings. Happy coding!
